UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 2: Comparing two places', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"To compare two places fairly, use the same criteria for both, such as location, climate, natural features, built features and how people use the place. Comparisons give evidence of similarity and difference and avoid saying one place is better.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite one similarity and one difference for the place you live and one in another state, using the same criteria for both.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBoth statements must use one category, such as climate or location, for both places, for example \"both are coastal\" and \"one is warmer in winter\".\n\nWATCH FOR\nComparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a fair comparison.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose one local place and one distant place. Complete a five-criteria grid with sources and write three sentences, with two similarities and one difference, using the same criteria for both.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"To compare two places fairly, use the same criteria for both, such as location, climate, natural features, built features and how people use the place. Comparisons give evidence of similarity and difference and avoid saying one place is better.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose one local place and one distant place. Complete a five-criteria grid with sources and write three sentences, with two similarities and one difference, using the same criteria for both. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using compare on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a. Correction: Both statements must use one category, such as climate or location, for both places, for example \"both are coastal\" and. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a fair comparison. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating compare on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with compare, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about compare with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for compare. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for compare. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Both statements must use one category, such as climate or location, for both places, for example \"both are coastal\" and"],"question":"Write one similarity and one difference for the place you live and one in another state, using the same criteria for both. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for compare; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Both statements must use one category, such as climate or location, for both places, for example \"both are coastal\" and","Careful use of criteria and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a","To compare two places fairly, use the same criteria for both, such as location, climate, natural features, built"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with compare?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named criteria and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply compare to new material and name the criteria and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a","Name criteria and explain why it matters only, without linking it to compare"],"question":"Which response best applies com