},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for compare?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name criteria and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Choose one local place and one distant place","Retype the worked example with no new example","Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a"],"question":"Which independent response best applies compare to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about compare with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating compare on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using compare on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for compare. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with compare, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a. Correction: Both statements must use one category, such as climate or location, for both places, for example \"both are coastal\" and. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w14-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise compare.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Geography","success_criteria":["I can use compare with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify criteria and explain why it matters.","I can practise similarity independently on a new prompt.","I can notice difference needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"To compare two places fairly, use the same criteria for both, such as location, climate, natural features, built features and how people use the place.","options":["To compare two places fairly, use the same criteria for both, such as location, climate, natural features, built","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about compare with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample comparison using climate: Cairns in Queensland is hot and humid with a wet summer; Hobart in Tasmania is cool with four seasons. Natural features: rainforest and reef near Cairns; mountain and harbour at Hobart. Similarity: both are coastal cities.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite one similarity and one difference for the place you live and one in another state, using the same criteria for both.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBoth statements must use one category, such as climate or location, for both places, for example \"both are coastal\" and \"one is warmer in winter\".","explain":"To compare two places fairly, use the same criteria for both, such as location, climate, natural features, built features and how people use the place. Comparisons give evidence of similarity and difference and avoid saying one place is better.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a fair comparison. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Comparing climate in two places.","caption":"Same criteria for both places.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cairns</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hot, humid, wet summer</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Hobart</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">cool, four seasons</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example comparison using climate: Cairns in Queensland is hot and humid with a wet summer; Hobart in Tasmania is cool wi Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example comparison using climate: Cairns in Queensland is hot and humid with a wet summer; Hobart in Tasmania is cool wi Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about compare with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample comparison using climate: Cairns in Queensland is hot and humid with a wet summer; Hobart in Tasmania is cool with four seasons. Natural features: rainforest and reef near Cairns; mountain and harbour at Hobart. Similarity: both are coastal cities. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Similarity and difference.","caption":"Evidence, not best or worst.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Both</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">coastal cities</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Different</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">reef or mountain</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about compare with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nComparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a fair comparison. Here is a more productive model: Example comparison using climate: Cairns in Queensland is hot and humid with a wet summer; Hobart in Tasmania is cool with four seasons. Natural features: rainforest and reef near Cairns; mountain and harbour at Hobart. Similarity: both are coastal cities.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBoth statements must use one category, such as climate or location, for both places, for example \"both are coastal\" and \"one is warmer in winter\".","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Comparing different things, such as one place''s weather with another''s food, or calling one place the best, is not a fair comparison. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the source or place","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about compare with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTo compare two places fairly, use the same criteria for both, such as location, climate, natural features, built features and how people use the place. Comparisons give evidence of similarity and difference and avoid saying one place is better. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 2: Comparing two places","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Places near and far","week":14,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample comparison using climate: Cairns in Queensland is hot and humid with a wet summer; Hobart in Tasmania is cool with four seasons. Natural features: rainforest and reef near Cairns; mountain and harbour at Hobart. Similarity: both are coastal cities.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Comparing climate in two places.","caption":"Same criteria for both places.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cairns</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hot, humid, wet summer</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Hobart</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">cool, four seasons</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Similarity and difference.","caption":"Evidence, not best or worst.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Both</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">coastal cities</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Different</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">reef or mountain</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-hsie-y3-w14-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
